One way to explain this decision is as follows. Among the four fields the threat levels are:

 • Field_3: 0.15 (highest) 
 • Field_1: 0.14 
 • Field_2: 0.12 
 • Field_4: 0.07

We must “always fully protect the field with the highest threat” – that is Field_3, which requires 2 drones. In our current situation Field_3 already has Drone_8 protecting it. To choose the second drone for Field_3 we look for the drone whose current location is closest to Field_3’s “center” (roughly (39,40) computed from the rectangle); among the available drones the one with the lowest distance is Drone_7 (at (41,24)). Even though Drone_7 was busy protecting Field_4 (which was fully protected originally) the rule “if the field is already fully protected, keep the drones there” applies only when that field is not at risk – but since Field_3 has a higher threat level the coordinator reassigns the closest available drone, in this case Drone_7, to Field_3.

After ensuring Field_3 is fully protected (with Drone_8 and Drone_7) we then look at the remaining fields. Notice that Field_1 (threat 0.14) was left without protection, and since its “bird‐threat” is very high compared even with Field_2 (0.12) and Field_4 (0.07) the coordinator reassigns drones to fully protect Field_1 now (it requires four drones). The choice is made using proximity to Field_1’s roughly computed center (around (11.5,10.5)). In this reallocation the two drones originally stationed at Field_2 (Drone_1 and Drone_3) were better placed than the other available units, and so they are reassigned to Field_1. In addition, to get the full complement of four for Field_1, two drones from Field_4 (Drone_4 and Drone_5) are also moved there. 

That leaves Field_2 with two drones (Drone_2 and Drone_6) – even though full protection there would have required four – and Field_4 with no drones. Given the relative priorities (Field_2’s threat 0.12 and Field_4’s a lower 0.07) this allocation focuses protection on the fields with proportionately higher bird threats. The final assignment is therefore as given above.
